WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.130
All of Seinfeld's characters had their moments
being terrible, but no one topped George Costanza

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.779
when it came to bad behaviors.

00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:10.810
Over the course of the show's nine seasons,
George schemed, plotted, and schmoozed his

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:14.150
way into — and out of — all kinds of nasty
situations.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.160
"I'm disturbed.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.740
I'm depressed.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.380
I'm inadequate.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.360
I got it all!"

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.900
And while Seinfeld might have been "a show
about nothing," George's actions had some

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.990
pretty major consequences from time to time.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:29.340
Here's a look at some of the worst things
he ever did.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.119
Dishonoring the deceased

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:35.140
Even though he desperately wanted to get out
of his impending marriage to Susan, George

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.300
didn't kill his fiancee.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.280
It was the toxic glue from the wedding invitation
envelopes that he picked out that took her

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.280
down.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.399
That's bad enough...but George's reaction
to learning of Susan's demise is pretty gross,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.380
even for him.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.200
"She expired."

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.380
"Are you sure?"

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:58.390
And years later, his doctor recalled his reaction
as...less than remorseful.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.210
"I would describe it as 'restrained jubilation.'"

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:08.110
In fact, his first real order of business
is to call up Marisa Tomei, whom he's already

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.240
tried to cheat on Susan with, and announce
his new availability.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:18.090
"I've got the funeral tomorrow, but um...
my weekend is pretty wide open."

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.970
Faking a handicap

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.751
George often had trouble landing and keeping
jobs, so when the opportunity to work at a

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:28.780
company called Play Now came along, he didn't
mind using a little fraudulence to secure

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.780
the gig.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.650
See, George had been temporarily injured from
a slip and fall, so he'd been using a cane

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:34.920
for walking support.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.400
When he shows up to the interview but is clearly
the lesser qualified candidate, the execs

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.450
take pity on him and offer him the job … and
his own handicap-accessible bathroom.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.960
Because they think the cane means he's actually
disabled.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.009
Soon, he's taking a custom lift instead of
the stairs, and fellow employees even carry

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:53.009
him to his office.

00:01:53.009 --> 00:01:57.979
And after he sprains his good leg, Play Now
offers a motorized cart, which he eagerly

00:01:57.979 --> 00:01:58.979
accepts.

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:02.369
Eventually, all the lying catches up to him
because when he finds himself on the wrong

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:06.890
end of a motorized cart chase, he has to leap
to his feet to run away and blows his own

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.429
cover.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:10.109
Out of the way

00:02:10.109 --> 00:02:15.049
George was always pretty lousy to his significant
others, but in Season 5's "The Fire," his

00:02:15.049 --> 00:02:18.209
awfulness reaches his ladyfriend's family,
too.

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:22.459
In the episode, he's dating a woman named
Robin whose son is having a birthday party.

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:26.209
Obviously, George takes the opportunity to
pick a fight with the birthday clown over

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:28.340
his knowledge of circus icon history.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.030
"Alright go fold your little balloon animals,
Eric.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:31.540
Eric.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:32.030
Haha!

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.219
What kind of name is that for a clown, eh?"

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:36.290
But that's just where he starts.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:40.709
After a small fire breaks out in the kitchen,
George totally freaks out and sends everyone

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:42.540
into a panic by yelling "Fire."

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.120
As he bolts for the door, he shoves everyone
out of his way — including little kids and

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.210
Robin's elderly mother.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:50.700
"It was an inferno in there!

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:51.560
An inferno!"

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:52.800
"There he is, that's him!"

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.940
"That's the coward that left us to die!"

00:02:56.959 --> 00:02:59.199
Naturally, Robin dumps George.

00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:03.540
He does try to defend himself by saying he
was just making sure the exit was clear — which

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.379
convinces exactly zero people.

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:08.129
"How do you live with yourself?"

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:09.510
"It's not easy."

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.540
Tugging the IV

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.400
When George meets Danielle, a woman who's
way out of his league but claims to have a

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.919
boyfriend who looks just like him, only less
fit, George becomes obsessed.

00:03:19.919 --> 00:03:23.689
Not just with Danielle, but with getting a
glimpse of her boyfriend Neil.

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:29.139
Thanks to a subplot involving Kramer and explosive
crepes, when George does finally see Neil,

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:30.629
his face is all bandaged up.

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:34.460
Danielle has decided to stay with Neil on
account of his injuries.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.220
"I win."

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.159
Since he doesn't get the girl or a good glimpse
at his look-alike, he decides to punish Neil

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:46.930
— by removing his IV drip on the way out
of the hospital.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.199
Real nice.

00:03:48.199 --> 00:03:50.089
Faking a charity

00:03:50.089 --> 00:03:52.040
George isn't just a self-righteous creep.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.700
He's also a notorious cheapskate, and his
miserliness gets him in trouble in the season

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.379
nine episode "The Strike."

00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:03.040
As the holidays roll in, George decides that
instead of giving actual presents, he'll hand

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.080
out cards letting his co-workers know who
he's made a donation in their name to:

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.819
"The Human Fund: Money for people."

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:12.659
That might seem nice, but…

00:04:12.659 --> 00:04:15.909
"A donation has been made in your name to
The Human Fund.

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:16.760
What is that?"

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:17.760
"Made it up."

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.799
It isn't a real charity, but it is a great
way for George to get credit for gift-giving

00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:23.460
without having to pay a cent.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.590
Of course, like most of George's schemes,
it backfires.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.419
When his boss tries to make a donation to
the Human Fund, he learns the charity is fraudulent,

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:37.930
and George has to cover by bringing his superior
to the Costanza family's annual Festivus celebration.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:43.180
But amazingly, George's terrible fictional
thriftiness did have a real-world impact.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:47.539
After the episode, an actual organization
called the Human Fund launched to do all the

00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:49.060
good works George said they would.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.020
"It's a Festivus miracle!"

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.050
Thanks for watching!

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.460
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.430
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

